# Hell in Heaven
Tamil title: சொர்க்கத்தில் நரகம்

Hell! The place where sinners who commit the five great sins are tortured.

Those who commit the sins of murder, theft, lust, gambling, and falsehood are said to go there and suffer.

Yet the Puranas themselves show that those who commit the five great sins live in luxury in heaven!

"What a wretched fate this is, what a life! What use are beauty and youth, or knowing dance and song? It is still a life of slavery! Shame! The degrading acts to which we must consent! Consent, indeed? We are driven into them! Did we perform penance upon penance and become celestial maidens only to attain this contemptible state? Rambha, this heavenly life is becoming more bitter to me by the day," said Tilottama, tears welling in her eyes.

"You call it bitter as neem, Tilottama; to me this life has become bitter as the etti fruit. We have seen cattle in their pens: their wounds are only upon their bodies. We possess bodies of pure gold, but in our hearts lie a thousand wounds. Good heavens! What degradation we sometimes have to endure! What blind lust we have to assist! Tilo, it would not matter if they pushed us into a fire; we would merely burn away. But they give us the task of pushing others into the fire!" Rambha was saying this when she saw Menaka approaching in the distance. "Look! Menaka is coming. See how exhausted she is! Poor woman, what mission can she have been sent upon? What suffering has she endured?" she asked with sympathy. Menaka reached the crystal steps where they sat, seated herself, and wiped away the pearls of sweat budding across her face.

Deodar trees rose high as if proudly declaring, "We are the trees of heaven." Flowers of many colours filled a grove as though saying, "The rainbow often competes with us, loses, and disappears." A little stream flowed as though inviting all to hear the sweetness that flute and yazh could offer. Fragrance was everywhere; the place wore a festive air. A pond held water so clear and free of moss that even a speck of dust falling into it would be visible. Like maidens delighting in their own loveliness in a crystal mirror, the flower-laden trees seemed to have acquired this pond solely to admire their beauty.

Celestial maidens occasionally sat upon the steps of that pond to pass the time. Knowing this, the gods promenaded there in the evenings as dandified gentlemen, choosing what sort of feast they desired. While seated there, the celestial women appeared to relieve their fatigue by discussing their individual afflictions and experiences.

What trouble could possibly befall them? An unbearable burden of family life? The grief of separation from the man who tied the marriage pendant after touching them? Could celestial maidens share the plight of earthly women who lament after gradually discovering that the bridegroom whom astrologers and brokers called a fortunate man whose touch turned everything to gold was merely a clod of earth? What could celestial maidens lack? They danced in Indra's court, dallied with the moon, and even Agni looked upon them with a cool stream's fondness. Nectar to eat, heavenly silks to wear, and flower-strewn paths beneath their feet! Who would not suppose that their life of pleasure lacked nothing? Only when they sat upon those crystal steps and spoke could one discover that their distress was enough to make the heart seethe.

Not a trace of anxiety could be seen upon the celestial maidens' faces. No one could tell that they carried a thousand sorrowful tales within. Their beauty was great enough to pour light into darkness. It is only on earth that gold turns to brass within four or six years!

"Kanaka? Truly? What a pity! She used to bound about like a fawn. Was her frolicking in the orchards any small thing? She feared nothing and would speak as though she could topple anyone she chose. If someone came angrily to attack her, a single smile would conquer him. What bewitching eyes she had! Parrots came to peck at those full red lips, mistaking them for kovvai fruit. Is this that same Kanaka? Grey hair! Lines of care across her face! The light has gone from her eyes. And those lips? Alas, must they lie so shrivelled? Her lively speech is gone; a sigh rises the moment she begins to speak. There is a weariness in her walk. Poor, pitiable woman! How old can she be now? Only thirty or thirty-two. Has she already acquired the appearance of Avvaiyar?" Such remarks belonged to earth, which was a realm of delight for these women. Yet that day they spoke wearily. Rambha pointed out the marks upon Menaka's cheek to Tilottama. "Poor thing," Tilottama said with pity.

"Menaka! You look utterly dishevelled, dear. Have you travelled very far?" Tilottama asked.

"Very far?" Menaka repeated and laughed, not from joy but in disgust. "Never mind how far I went, Tilottama. Do you know what I suffered?"

"What command were you given?" asked Rambha.

"A command! You foolish girl, do they ever command us to impart wisdom, teach love, or show anyone the path to understanding the Lord's auspicious virtues? We must induce people to consent to sin. We must push them into the pit of lust and fill them with the delirium of desire. We must turn the man engaged in severe penance into a libertine. We must spin like a top a man who, through many penances, has acquired the power to perform many wonders. We must kindle the fire of lust in the heart of the sage who delights in watching the sacrificial flame leap high! Is that not the command given to us? Are we seated here to perform work that cultivates virtue? The arrow in a hunter's hand is used only to kill a dove or a fawn, not to make a bud blossom. Are we not just like that arrow?" Menaka spoke in a voice mingling anger and exhaustion.

"I was just saying the same thing to Rambha. I dislike this existence more with every passing day. We see women on earth. They may not be golden vines, but how honourably they live! They themselves receive love. Even a man who has planted his banner of victory in all eight directions becomes like a child and coos before his beloved. Menaka, they have a thousand and eight troubles, yet love becomes a great medicine that cures them all. And here we are, women, and celestial women at that! We wear parijata flowers and fragrant sandal paste. We have silks and many ornaments unobtainable on earth. We dance like flowering vines and sing like birds. We have flower gardens in which to stroll in delight. Yet is there any way for noble thought to blossom in our hearts? Do we receive an opportunity to teach the world a higher path?

"Embrace and kiss him! Hold him and make him your slave! Cast a sidelong glance and sway your waist! Make him fall at your feet and push him into the pit of lust! He may speak harshly, but do not keep your distance. Though sparks fly from his eyes, let a smile flower upon your lips. Do not fear his angry gaze. Cast the net of desire and capture him! Bring the man who thinks only of penance, who believes that seeking and desiring women is sinful, to the point of caressing your flower-soft feet! He may now make the Vedas resound, but through your arts he must worship you as jewel, deer, and emerald. You must enslave him in that fashion. I do not care how you do it. He may be a savage with no taste, an innocent without experience, or a man exhausted by indulgence. Whether you enchant him with smiling speech, melt him with a sindhu song, conquer him through dance, or approach, embrace him tightly, and enslave him with nectar from your lips, I do not know. His ascetic guise must be destroyed. He must fling away his staff of yoga and roll upon the ground of pleasure. Go! Go! The man praised as sacred and venerable must become a libertine. Employ whatever method your ingenuity demands and return here only after you have won. Do not fear danger or worry that he may curse you. Let your youthful, voluptuous body become weapons brimming with love to make him a sinner. If you stand upon the field and shoot the arrow of your eyes, his ascetic discipline will collapse. Is this new to you? Have you only begun this work today? Are you not an enchantress whose arts could melt even a stone image? I have complete confidence in you. Go forth and return victorious!

"Is this not the command issued to us here? Rambha, I swear upon the Lord: had I known that this was to be our life, I would never have agreed to come here," said Tilottama.

"Who will ever learn of our anguish? Let it be. This must be the fruit of some evil deed of ours; what can we do? Tell us what mission you have just returned from," Rambha asked. Tilottama drew Menaka close and embraced her. Turning to Rambha she said, "You wicked imp! Can you not see what she has suffered merely by looking at her? Why keep prodding her with questions? My dear, do not grieve," and tried to comfort Menaka.

"Tilottama, I thought that this great sage would at least know the difference between plucking a flower and crushing and throwing it away. But reading the four Vedas and six shastras again and again had only made his heart coarse. He fondled me, calling me his golden one, but embraced me as though I were a statue of iron. He said my lips were flowers, yet did he know even how to pluck and enjoy a flower gently and skilfully? Alas, I was caught by him and ruined. I became a mother. Do you know that?" she asked.

"You queen of beauty! You have become a mother? Listen, Rambha, Menaka has become a mother! Where is the baby, dear? What child was it, a boy or a girl? It must have been beautiful. Did it have one face, or six or seven? Did it sing or run as soon as it was born? Tell me, golden image! Tell me, where is the baby?" Tilottama asked eagerly.

"A beautiful girl. I left her in the forest," Menaka replied sorrowfully.

"In the forest or in a palace?" asked Rambha.

"In the forest, girl, the forest! My flood of joy, my beloved child, is there in the wilderness! What else could I do? My command was to destroy Vishvamitra's penance. He was said to be quick-tempered, and he was a brute as well. I danced until my legs ached, firing arrows of lust at the man engaged in severe penance. He opened his eyes. He opened his mouth to curse me, then saw me. His anger subsided and desire surged. The green grassy bank became our bed. Parrot and myna sang the auspicious wedding song, herds of deer danced, and our festival of love reached its happy fulfilment. Happy, that is, for him. As for me, I became a mother to a girl," Menaka explained.

"Look, Rambha! That sage had performed penance for ages, seeking the Supreme. He must have stood in water, sat amid fire, balanced upon a needle point, and undertaken severe penance while vowing to consume nothing but air. He must have conducted every variety of sacrifice. Yet he received no boon. Look at Menaka! She granted him a divine vision and gave him a boon too," Tilottama said.

"Vishvamitra must have swept the child into his arms, kissed the crown of her head, and crooned: 'Fruit of nectar borne by the forest beauty I found in the wilderness! Embodiment of art who will delight epic poets and master painters and make imagination flow! Fruit of my penance! Has the Vedic chant rising beside the sacrificial pit taken your form and come to enrich my life? I sought eternal bliss. I sought the Pure One in order to obtain it. Thinking its abode was heaven, I wearied myself looking upward. Pleased by my penance, the protector of all worlds has bestowed his boon in your form! Child dripping beauty! Rare treasure I received in the forest!'" Rambha imagined, not teasing but genuinely delighted.

Tears rolled down Menaka's cheeks. "Life in the forest had turned that villain's heart to stone. 'I have become a libertine! My severe penance is ruined! How can I approach the presence of God with this stain upon me?' he said, and refused even to touch my treasure, the sacred joy ripened through his own play of love. In that same forest a tiger plays with its cubs and affection exists even inside a serpent's hole. Yet no love welled up in that sinner seeking heaven for the child he had fathered. The moment he saw my beauty, his penance vanished; the moment he had obtained pleasure, his mind returned to penance. What a strange mind!

"Like a robber who blocks the road, beats a traveller, steals his goods, and then hurries away, he took what he wanted from me, remembered the Vedas he had learned, and shut his eyes tight. 'I am a celestial maiden. Do you not know that it is improper for me to bring children here? On earth she will become a beauty of the world. Venerable one, hold her in your hands! Look at her lovely eyes! See her blue-lotus eyes: solid gold has taken the form of a child!' I said, trying to awaken his affection. But he became a man with a heart of stone.

"Was he truly incapable of emotion? Only moments before, he lay grinning and calling me swan, gold, nectar, and fruit. Though I protested, 'This does not befit your station; do not commit this offence,' he caressed my feet and poured out desire, saying, 'Do they not scatter flowers along the paths of heaven? Otherwise might these feet be hurt?' Once his business was finished, he began threatening me: 'I am a man of severe penance. Woman, what have you done?' What could I do? Even a hen rages when it sees a hawk circling to seize its chicks. But what could I accomplish? I had to leave the baby in that vast forest where tigers and other beasts roam!" Menaka sobbed.

"Menaka, do not cry. That forest will contain hunters as well as ascetics performing penance with closed eyes. They will not fail to show the baby affection. The fault may not belong to that sage alone, dear. A man who sees a tree heavy with delicious fruit climbs it or strikes it with a stone to obtain the fruit. He eats and delights in it, then forgets within a moment the tree that gave him its flavour. That is the way of men, particularly the men whom we meet! On their road to meeting God and attaining 'bliss,' they encounter us midway and obtain pleasure. Then they reject us and resume their sacred journey. We become flowers cast away and fruit crushed and discarded.

"It is foolishness that leaves us subject to such cruelty. The delusion of life in heaven and a vision of the Lord of lords has brought us to this state. People on earth have old age, disease, and death. We are immortal. Our youth never changes, our beauty never fades, and we can assume any form we imagine. This moment we may be in heaven, and the next upon earth. We swell with pride that we possess such greatness, and this is the consequence. We become the women of one man after another, unable to receive anyone's lasting love, renounce both the lover who embraces us and the child we bear, and return here to shed tears. Do we not choose for ourselves this 'hellish' life in heaven? Had we even a little power to think and act, would we have consented to it? We endure it and bow our heads, believing it the command of the Lord of all," Tilottama said with a deep sigh.

"On earth they offer such marvellous teachings: do not abandon the woman you married; do not unite with every woman you see and ruin yourself; do not wander consumed by lust; do not violate helpless maidens; do not wallow in sensual luxury. Tilottama, they praise rare virtues such as morality, family life, refraining from desiring another's wife, and avoiding deceitful conduct. They despise and punish the person who departs from the moral path. We reject that earthly realm where people live with such character, come here, and wallow in the mire of lust as though it were divine. When I think of it, I do not know whether to weep or laugh.

"The people of earth believe that the Lord throws an immoral person into hell. They fear it and conduct themselves with reverence. Some who claim to be companions closely connected with God make immorality their special distinction and let five men enjoy one beauty, while ordinary people preserve morality. We call such a place base, yet live here doing acts that even the lowest people there would be ashamed to commit. What a marvel! We do not merely call earth inferior; people on earth are taught that heaven is superior. That causes me pain mingled with amusement," said Menaka.

"Menaka, even women who were supremely virtuous on earth, who never disobeyed their husbands and could bear every hardship and loss for them, would need to remain in heaven for only ten days. They would either fall at someone's feet in tears and plead, 'Turn us upside down and cast us back to earth; we do not want this immortal existence writhing with injustice and obscenity,' or they would gradually be pushed, without even knowing it, into the abyss of blind lust. Poor creatures! Unaware of the degradation here, they conduct worship there, fall at the feet of priests, undertake severe fasts and vows, and perform penance in the hope of reaching heaven.

"Yes. They imagine heaven to be a realm where one worships the divine names of the Lord of lords, visits Kailasa in the morning and Vaikuntha in the evening, and obtains supreme bliss; a world where one converses daily with the Pure One and rejoices. Do they know of the frenzied sexual revelry conducted here?"

"To prevent that truth from becoming known, the schemers here have sent a company called Puranic storytellers to earth and instructed them to fabricate whatever they please about the greatness of the gods. Drinking until one babbles, sporting with any available woman, and violating another man's wife are all degraded acts. Yet on earth the Puranic expositors give philosophical interpretations to every one of them, call them divine glory, and delude the people. The very deeds regarded in their world as the five great sins, deeds that honourable people shrink from, deeds for which a person is condemned as degraded and excluded from society, are performed here by the gods in a frenzy. Instead of feeling disgust, they call them God's sacred sports, burn camphor, and slap their cheeks in worship. What a fate!"

"The Puranic writers have intoxicated them to that extent."

"Yes. Once, while listening from the sky to a Hari-katha performance, even I was deluded for a moment."

"They dispatch Puranic storytellers to deceive the people of earth, while deluding us by calling this a blessed residence in heaven."

"They even call our sexual antics sacred service. How else could they describe them? We see it ourselves: those celebrated here as the principal deities refuse even to think about or respect morality. Good heavens, are the obscenities committed by Maha Vishnu few?"

"Poor Narada! Merely thinking of what he suffered after being caught by Vishnu makes me laugh."

"Why did that mischief-maker assume the form of a woman? The moment he saw a new woman, he engaged in sacred service."

"They say sixty sons were born from that obscene union."

"The Puranic storyteller told the people of earth that celebrating the names of those sixty children as the sixty years was a sacred act, and the people believed him."

"Look at the injustice! It is like building a temple to a burglar's crowbar and telling those who were robbed to worship it."

"What will these gods not do? They have made people on earth believe that worship of the Shiva-linga itself is especially meritorious."

"Yes. Every obscenity performed here is transformed there into a gift from the gods."

"Do you know why Maha Vishnu assumed the form of Mohini?"

"The god and Hara united; Ayyanar was born. On earth they celebrate Ayyanar in magnificent fashion."

"That is not what I am asking. Do you know why Maha Vishnu assumed the form of Mohini? To threaten us: 'Celestial maidens, if you do not obey our commands, we lose nothing. We know many other methods. We do not need your favour. We can become Mohini and render "service" to one another.' That is why Maha Vishnu took Mohini's form."

"That may well be so. When they command us, 'Destroy that sage's penance; embrace this king and enslave him,' suppose we object that it is difficult and that we have plenty of other work. Maha Vishnu could simply say, 'Is that all? We need none of your help. Look, I shall become Mohini myself and break this ascetic's concentration.'"

"Let these gods freely retain that great service as their monopoly. It is enough if they let us leave this place. Can we not find an honourable man suited to us on earth and pass our lives with him? We want neither nectar nor this obscene existence."

"All we can do is sit here and mutter like this. When the moment arrives to make a decision, temptation is born in us again. We find our anklets, fasten them to our feet, and dance in Indra's court. Before the resulting weariness passes, if some god grins at us we become his prey.

"On earth there was a woman named Madhavi. She belonged to the community of courtesans, and her allotted fate was to become some wealthy gentleman's mistress, delight him, and pass her life. Along with beauty, youth, and allure, she possessed perfect mastery of the arts. When she danced in the king's court, everyone cried out in admiration. Kovalan, a young merchant, desired her love and obtained it. The two enjoyed a life of pleasure. Later he became angry with her without cause and left her; after all, she was only a courtesan. They call her a mass of sin and her house a place where blame accumulates!

"Do you know what that same Madhavi did when she heard that the beloved who had delighted her had left her and was subsequently killed through a terrible turn of events? Here we pass from Budha to Brihaspati and from Surya to Chandra, changing hands in this fashion. Did that beautiful dancer do the same? She said: 'He was the man dear to my heart and therefore my husband. After he has died, Madhavi too ought to die; that alone is virtue. But I have a duty to give the child growing in my womb to the world. I remain alive only until that duty is fulfilled.'

"After Kovalan's death, Madhavi decided that it was no longer righteous to continue the life of love in which she had played and cooed with him, danced like a splendid peacock, heard him call her tender one and parrot in his sweet voice, savoured the breeze and nectar with him, and danced like a flowering vine while he played the spotless yazh and sang a sweet melody beneath the evening moon. She had been a laughing, playful beauty; a jewel among women who cast meaningful glances, poured forth the nine emotions, and danced; a woman who discovered what adornment would please the lord of her heart and poured out allure and the delight of love accordingly. Yet she resolved that Madhavi must now die.

"That flower-faced woman renounced flowers. Her eyes renounced their radiance, her body its lustre, and her dark hair its oil. Her smile disappeared. In a single day she became an Avvaiyar. She emptied her mansion, gave all her possessions to the poor, and entered the Buddhist faith. This happened on earth! Such nobility arose in a courtesan. Yet these noble beings here employ the Puranic storytellers to tell people on earth that they can acquire merit and come to reside in heaven, while leaving us to flounder in the mire of lust."

"Are we alone floundering in that mire?"

"It pains me too when I think that these fools consider the celestial world superior. One day I even feel that we ought to tell the people of earth about it," said Tilottama.

"But we are not fortunate enough to do that, Tilottama. Whenever they send us to earth, it is only to ruin someone and destroy morality," Menaka said.

"What if all of us rebel and depart for earth?" Tilottama asked.

"Could we do it? All we know is to speak in a breathy voice, saying, 'My lord! My master! My love, my very life!' to stand with one leg raised and dance, singing, 'Upon the dancing vine is a lovely flower; upon my body is a flower of delight'; to embrace and kiss, to sing, and then to remember what we suffered and sob. Beyond that, do we know how to ask why, speak in opposition, or make a revolution?" Menaka thundered.

Rambha emerged from deep thought. "We cannot do it, but such intellectual strength is now spreading widely on earth. People are exposing the ugliness of the place in which we live and reforming humankind. A revolutionary wind is blowing," she said.

The bell rang with a peal. All three started in alarm.

"Today I dance in Indra's court," Tilottama said as she rose.

"Today I must perform the service of cooling Indra's heat," said Rambha.

"And you?" they both asked Menaka.

"Me? Today I am going to Yama," she replied with a laugh.

"What is this about Rambha and Menaka?" my wife asked, tapping me awake, and my dream dissolved.

* * *

---NOTES---
`பஞ்சமாபாதகம்` denotes the five great or mortal sins. The article itself names murder, theft, lust, gambling, and falsehood.

`மோகலாகிரி` is a period expression for the intoxication or delirium of desire, not a place-name.

`ஒளவையார் கோலம்` invokes the traditional aged appearance of the poet Avvaiyar as a metaphor for becoming prematurely old and careworn.

`கண்ணம்பு` literally means an arrow of the eyes: a seductive glance treated as a weapon.

The beautiful daughter whom Menaka leaves in the forest in the Vishvamitra episode alludes to Shakuntala, although the source does not name her here. The translation likewise does not insert her name into the narrative.

The dialogue refers satirically to Puranic accounts involving Vishnu as Mohini, Shiva, Ayyanar, and the sixty names of the calendrical years. These claims are translated as assertions made by the characters, without treating them as independently verified history or theology.

The Madhavi passage invokes the *Silappatikaram*: Madhavi renounces wealth and adornment and enters the Buddhist faith after learning of Kovalan's death. The source's extended comparison is retained in full.

The Tamil source combines fourteen scan images across two website parts. Obvious OCR substitutions, split words, page-footer debris, and a false final token were corrected against the scans; printed period diction, spellings, satire, and mythological framing were retained.
